2026-09-10 claude-code 2.1.266 -> 2.1.267; no hook/plugin/skill/subagent schema changes (2.1.267 adds maxEffortLevel setting + --system-prompt-snapshot off, fixes marketplace path containment on macOS/Linux, and makes 'effort:' frontmatter apply on pinned-default models - review-crew agents already declare effort: high, no change needed); no function-hooks signal; all 7 plugins validate --strict; free 121/121, pro 23/23; no code change.
2026-09-11 claude-code 2.1.267 -> 2.1.268; no hook/plugin/skill/subagent schema changes; two changes touched us: plain WebFetch deny/ask rules no longer cover Artifact tool reads+updates (setup-audit 0.1.2 rubric now deducts for a WebFetch exfil rule with no Artifact rule beside it, and the inventory surfaces both), and /plugin installs now take effect on closing the menu so /reload-plugins is no longer needed (install docs corrected). No function-hooks signal in the changelog or docs, but plugin validate --strict runs the engine's module scan and it refuses anchorwatch-mod 0.0.1: a registration whose value is kept, which is how it feature-detected .catch — fixed in 0.0.2 by chaining .catch at the call site; scan rules documented in the mod README and pinned in its tests (issue opened). Pre-existing since 2.1.267, not a 2.1.268 regression; the mod is not in CI's validate job, which is why earlier runs counted 7 plugins. All 8 plugins validate --strict; free 121/121, pro 23/23, mod 182/182.
2026-09-12 claude-code 2.1.268 -> 2.1.269; no hook/plugin/skill/subagent schema changes; two engine changes touched us: an Edit() deny rule and the working-directory write check now cover the file a Bash `tee` writes (they did not before), and a deny/ask rule starting with `!` now applies only within the settings source that wrote it (a bare `!` is ignored) — no Anchorwatch rule or doc uses `!` negations, so nothing to correct there. The `tee` fix exposed the same hole in our own rules: secret-files only ever saw Edit/Write, so `tee .env`, `> .env`, `cp x .env`, `sed -i .env` and `dd of=.env` walked past it. New block rule secret-write closes it (anchorwatch 0.1.2), the path list moved into lib.sh as aw_is_secret_path so the two rules cannot drift, and the mod ports it (0.0.3). setup-audit 0.1.3 grades the same gap in user settings (Edit deny + broad Bash allow) and its inventory now reports `claude --version`, which two rubric items depend on. Also new in 2.1.269: `claude plugin eval` (scored eval suites for a plugin, JSON + HTML report) — not used yet, candidate for pinning the rules as evals rather than only fixtures. No function-hooks signal in the changelog or docs. All 8 plugins validate --strict; free 138/138 (jq, node, python3), pro 23/23, mod 216/216. Not covered by secret-write: copying a secret OUT (`cp .env /tmp/x`) is still only a warn-level concern via outside-project on the file tools.
2026-09-13 claude-code 2.1.269 -> 2.1.270; a single-bullet release ("fixed read-only git commands in Bash unexpectedly asking for permission after a session had been running for a while", a 2.1.269 regression) — engine-side permission caching, no hook/plugin/skill/subagent schema change and nothing Anchorwatch relies on: our Bash guard denies destructive commands at PreToolUse and never reaches for the engine's read-only-git allow path. The 2.1.269 `tee` fix our 0.1.2 secret-write rule and setup-audit 0.1.3 rubric text cite is untouched, so those stay accurate. No function-hooks signal in the changelog or in the hooks/plugins-reference docs (still JSON-only hooks: command, http, mcp_tool, prompt, agent). All 8 plugins validate --strict; free 138/138 under jq, node and python3, pro 23/23, mod 216/216. No code change. Still open from 2.1.269: plugins-reference documents `experimental.evals` as the plugin.json field `claude plugin eval` reads, so pinning the rules as an eval suite alongside the fixtures remains the backlog candidate.
